  <section id="best-for">
    <h2>Who the Cabins at Fort Wilderness are best for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Families of six who want a kitchen and a cabin in the woods,</strong> with a boat to Magic Kingdom, without booking a campsite or bringing an RV.</li>
      <li><strong>People who like Fort Wilderness</strong> — the internal buses, the trails, the Settlement and the Meadow — and want to stay on this campus.</li>
      <li><strong>Groups who prefer one cabin to two Value hotel rooms,</strong> if the rate works for the dates.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Think twice if…</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>You want to walk out of your room to a monorail or to a park gate.</strong> Magic Kingdom is a boat, plus an internal bus to the dock from many loops.</li>
      <li><strong>You assumed this was still the older Moderate cabin.</strong> Official 2026 listings treat these as a Deluxe Villa product, with no extra-adult charge up to six, at about $526 to $1,041 a night at full price, tax included.</li>
      <li><strong>A bunk bed is a problem for your group.</strong> The third sleeping space is a bunk, not a second pull-down or a sofa in the 2026 bed list.</li>
    </ul>
    <span class="ryan">[Ryan: who you think this resort is really for, from your own stays. Two or three sentences.]</span>
  </section>

